PART 2 - ASSISTANT ANSWERS / TECHNICAL GUIDANCE RECOVERABLE FROM THE CHAT
======================================================================

A. ESP32 / HEX BOARD CONNECTION CONCEPT
- The ESP32 DevKit V1 generates servo-style PWM signals for the hex board.
- Five control channels are used:
  AIL = aileron / roll
  ELE = elevator / pitch
  THR = throttle
  RUDD = rudder / yaw
  AUX = auxiliary channel
- Standard servo PWM discussed:
  approximately 50 Hz, 20 ms frame, pulse width from 1000 to 2000 microseconds.
- A common ground between ESP32 and the hex board is mandatory.
- The ESP32 GPIO signal wires go to the corresponding signal inputs on the hex board.
- The exact OUT1-OUT8 mapping on the hex board must be verified from its labels/manual; do not assume the board's output numbering from a generic diagram.

B. ORIGINAL PIN CONFIGURATION
The first RMT sketch used:
- AIL: GPIO14, RMT_CHANNEL_0
- ELE: GPIO27, RMT_CHANNEL_1
- THR: GPIO26, RMT_CHANNEL_2
- RUDD: GPIO25, RMT_CHANNEL_3
- AUX: GPIO33, RMT_CHANNEL_4

C. UPDATED PIN CONFIGURATION REQUESTED LATER
The user explicitly changed the five GPIO pins to:
- AIL: GPIO27
- ELE: GPIO26
- THR: GPIO25
- RUDD: GPIO33
- AUX: GPIO32

This is the later requested pin map. It supersedes the original GPIO14/27/26/25/33 map for the five-channel configuration, unless the user explicitly changes it again.

D. RMT COMPILATION ISSUE
- The user encountered a legacy RMT deprecation warning with Arduino-ESP32 core 3.x.
- The compile error specifically said `ServoChannel` had no member named `rmt`.
- That error means the code used `s.rmt` while the struct field was named `channel` (or otherwise declared differently).
- The warning about `driver/rmt.h` being deprecated is separate from the missing-member compilation error.
- ESP-IDF / Arduino-ESP32 3.x prefers the newer RMT TX API (`driver/rmt_tx.h`) over the legacy driver.
- The working sketch in the conversation continued to use the legacy RMT API, and the user later reported that the standalone version worked.

E. SERIAL MONITOR DEBUGGING
- The user repeatedly reported that serial input was not appearing.
- The boot log showed the ESP32 starting normally and printing `ESP32 RMT SERIAL ONLINE`.
- A visibility-test sketch was proposed to echo received characters and print complete commands after Enter.
- The key debugging distinction was between:
  1. seeing the startup banner,
  2. seeing characters echoed as they are typed,
  3. seeing a complete parsed command after Enter.
- Serial Monitor line ending should match the parser. The parser shown in the working sketch accepts CR and LF.
- The user later saw `ARMED`, which indicated that a sketch with ARM command parsing was running and had received an ARM command. It did not prove that a different visibility-test sketch had been uploaded.

F. HEX BOARD INPUT WARNINGS
- The hex screen initially showed `NO ROLL INPUT`.
- AIL corresponds to roll.
- After an AIL-only test, the screen showed `NO YAW INPUT` and other no-signal warnings.
- The recommended diagnostic was to drive all five channels continuously at 1500 microseconds.
- The user later asked specifically to set all five outputs to 1500 microseconds.
- A 1500 microsecond pulse is the center position for a normal RC channel. For throttle, however, it is approximately half-scale, not zero throttle.
- A 1000 microsecond pulse is the usual low-throttle command.
- The earlier assistant suggested a possible 3.3 V versus 5 V signal-level issue, but this was not proven. Do not treat it as confirmed without measuring or checking the hex board's input specifications.

G. WORKING SERIAL/RMT SKETCH PROVIDED BY THE USER
The user supplied this code and described it as working, but only when controlled through Serial:

```cpp
#include "driver/rmt.h"

/* ================= CONFIG ================= */
#define RMT_CLK_DIV       80        // 80 MHz / 80 = 1 MHz → 1 µs per tick
#define SERVO_MIN         1000
#define SERVO_MAX         2000
#define SERVO_SAFE        1000
#define SERVO_FRAME       20000     // 20 ms = 50 Hz

#define SERIAL_TIMEOUT    2000      // ms
#define THR_RAMP_STEP     5         // µs per loop
#define LOOP_DELAY        10        // ms

/* ================= GPIO MAP ================= */
#define PIN_AIL   14
#define PIN_ELE   27
#define PIN_THR   26
#define PIN_RUDD  25
#define PIN_AUX   33

/* ================= STRUCT ================= */
struct ServoChannel {
  rmt_channel_t channel;
  gpio_num_t pin;
};

/* ================= CHANNEL MAP ================= */
ServoChannel servos[5] = {
  {RMT_CHANNEL_0, GPIO_NUM_14}, // AIL
  {RMT_CHANNEL_1, GPIO_NUM_27}, // ELE
  {RMT_CHANNEL_2, GPIO_NUM_26}, // THR
  {RMT_CHANNEL_3, GPIO_NUM_25}, // RUDD
  {RMT_CHANNEL_4, GPIO_NUM_33}  // AUX
};

/* ================= STATE ================= */
uint16_t target[5] = {1500, 1500, 1000, 1500, 1500};
uint16_t output[5] = {1500, 1500, 1000, 1500, 1500};

bool armed = false;
unsigned long lastSerial = 0;

rmt_item32_t rmtItem;

/* ================= RMT ================= */
void setupServo(const ServoChannel& s) {
  rmt_config_t cfg = {};
  cfg.rmt_mode = RMT_MODE_TX;
  cfg.channel = s.channel;
  cfg.gpio_num = s.pin;
  cfg.mem_block_num = 1;
  cfg.clk_div = RMT_CLK_DIV;

  cfg.tx_config.loop_en = false;              // we refresh manually
  cfg.tx_config.carrier_en = false;
  cfg.tx_config.idle_output_en = true;
  cfg.tx_config.idle_level = RMT_IDLE_LEVEL_LOW;

  rmt_config(&cfg);
  rmt_driver_install(s.channel, 0, 0);
}

void setServoPulse(const ServoChannel& s, uint16_t pulse) {
  pulse = constrain(pulse, SERVO_MIN, SERVO_MAX);

  rmtItem.level0 = 1;
  rmtItem.duration0 = pulse;
  rmtItem.level1 = 0;
  rmtItem.duration1 = SERVO_FRAME - pulse;

  rmt_write_items(s.channel, &rmtItem, 1, true);
}

/* ================= SAFETY ================= */
void emergencyStop() {
  armed = false;
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    target[i] = SERVO_SAFE;
    output[i] = SERVO_SAFE;
    setServoPulse(servos[i], SERVO_SAFE);
  }
  Serial.println("\n*** EMERGENCY STOP ***");
}

/* ================= SERIAL ================= */
void processCommand(String cmd) {
  cmd.trim();
  cmd.toUpperCase();
  lastSerial = millis();

  Serial.print("\nCMD: ");
  Serial.println(cmd);

  if (cmd == "ARM") {
    armed = true;
    Serial.println("ARMED");
    return;
  }

  if (cmd == "DISARM" || cmd == "STOP") {
    emergencyStop();
    Serial.println("DISARMED");
    return;
  }

  int eq = cmd.indexOf('=');
  if (eq < 0) {
    Serial.println("ERR FORMAT");
    return;
  }

  int value = constrain(cmd.substring(eq + 1).toInt(), SERVO_MIN, SERVO_MAX);

  if      (cmd.startsWith("AIL="))  target[0] = value;
  else if (cmd.startsWith("ELE="))  target[1] = value;
  else if (cmd.startsWith("THR="))  target[2] = value;
  else if (cmd.startsWith("RUDD=")) target[3] = value;
  else if (cmd.startsWith("AUX="))  target[4] = value;
  else {
    Serial.println("ERR UNKNOWN");
    return;
  }

  Serial.println("OK");
}

void handleSerial() {
  static String buffer;

  while (Serial.available()) {
    char c = Serial.read();
    Serial.write(c);

    if (c == '\n' || c == '\r') {
      if (buffer.length()) processCommand(buffer);
      buffer = "";
    } else {
      buffer += c;
    }
  }
}

/* ================= SETUP ================= */
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(1000);

  Serial.println("\nESP32 RMT SERVO CONTROLLER READY");

  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    setupServo(servos[i]);
    setServoPulse(servos[i], SERVO_SAFE);
  }

  emergencyStop();
}

/* ================= LOOP ================= */
void loop() {
  handleSerial();

  // FAILSAFE
  if (armed && millis() - lastSerial > SERIAL_TIMEOUT) {
    Serial.println("\nFAILSAFE TIMEOUT");
    emergencyStop();
  }

  // RAMP OUTPUTS
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (!armed && i == 2) continue; // block throttle when disarmed

    if (output[i] < target[i]) output[i] += THR_RAMP_STEP;
    if (output[i] > target[i]) output[i] -= THR_RAMP_STEP;

    setServoPulse(servos[i], output[i]);
  }

  delay(LOOP_DELAY);
}
```

H. STANDALONE MODIFICATION
The assistant then supplied a standalone-capable version with:
