======================================================================
PART 4 - TECHNICAL CORRECTIONS / THINGS NOT TO ASSUME
======================================================================

1. Do not assume the ESC beep pattern has one universal meaning. It is manufacturer- and firmware-specific.

2. Do not assume the hex board's battery terminal is a 5 V terminal just because a servo-header rail measures 5 V. Read the exact board labels and specifications.

3. Do not apply 12 V to a 5 V rail. A wrong connection can destroy the hex board, ESP32, ESC BEC, or connected peripherals.

4. Do not connect multiple ESC BEC red wires together unless the ESC/BEC documentation explicitly permits parallel operation. Use one known suitable BEC or a properly designed power-distribution arrangement.

5. Do not automatically arm a flight controller at boot. Use a deliberate arming action, low-throttle verification, and a reliable disarm/failsafe mechanism.

6. Do not command full throttle at ESC power-up. Establish and hold the required low-throttle signal before powering the ESC.

7. Do not install propellers during bench tests, ESC calibration, motor mapping, or direction checks.

8. Do not assume the fifth motor belongs in the Quad-X mixer. A standard Quad-X uses four lift motors. Determine the fifth motor's function separately.

9. Do not treat the generic Quad-X mixer signs or motor rotation order as final until the actual frame orientation, motor numbering, and rotation directions are verified.

10. A multimeter's average voltage reading on a PWM signal is not a reliable way to prove pulse width or signal integrity. Use an oscilloscope or logic analyzer if available, or verify with a known-good RC tester.

======================================================================
PART 5 - RECOMMENDED NEXT STEPS
======================================================================

Priority 1: Identify the ESCs
- Record the brand, model, current rating, and firmware if known.
- Photograph the ESC label and its wiring.
- Find the manufacturer's startup and calibration instructions.
- Determine whether each ESC has an internal BEC and its output voltage/current rating.

Priority 2: Verify the hex board power pins
- Record the exact board model and all labels near the battery input and servo headers.
- With all power disconnected, inspect continuity only as a preliminary check.
- Confirm the board's battery-input voltage range from its documentation before applying 12 V.
- Confirm whether the board routes battery voltage, regulated 5 V, or both to its headers.

Priority 3: Verify one ESC at a time
- Remove every propeller.
- Use one ESC and one motor only.
- Use the latest confirmed pin map only after confirming the code and wiring match.
- Connect signal and ground correctly.
- Provide a stable low-throttle signal before applying ESC battery power.
- Wait for the documented arming confirmation.
- Do not ramp throttle until the ESC arms successfully.

Priority 4: Verify signal electrically
- Confirm the pulse is near the ESC's required low-throttle value.
- Confirm the repetition rate is what the ESC expects.
- Confirm ESP32 ground and ESC signal ground are common.
- Check whether the ESC input accepts 3.3 V logic. Do not add a level shifter unless the ESC/board specifications or measurements show one is required.
- If 5 V logic is required, use a suitable buffer such as a 5 V-powered HCT-family buffer with appropriate wiring. Verify the exact part and circuit before connecting.

Priority 5: Confirm motor layout
- Label the physical motors M1-M4 for the Quad-X frame.
- Confirm front direction and each motor's position.
- Confirm each motor's rotation direction against the frame/propeller specification.
- Test rotation direction with props removed.
- Keep the fifth motor separate until its purpose is defined.

Priority 6: Build the flight-control software safely
- Keep a known-good five-channel PWM baseline.
- Use a deliberate arm/disarm state machine.
- Enforce throttle-low before arming.
- Add a command timeout / failsafe that drives throttle to the safe minimum.
- Add a hardware kill switch if possible.
- Use the newer RMT TX API for Arduino-ESP32 core 3.x if retaining RMT.
- Add Quad-X mixing only after the signal outputs and ESC startup are proven.
- Do not attempt flight without a suitable IMU, stabilization controller, tested failsafe, and proper airframe testing.

======================================================================
PART 6 - SHORT PROJECT SUMMARY
======================================================================

The project uses an ESP32 DevKit V1 to generate five RC-style PWM control signals for a hexTronik/hex board. The channels are AIL, ELE, THR, RUDD, and AUX. The initial GPIO map was 14, 27, 26, 25, and 33. The user later requested the updated map 27, 26, 25, 33, and 32.

The user worked through serial-monitor debugging, RMT API/struct compilation errors, missing channel warnings on the hex board, and output testing. A five-channel standalone PWM version was eventually reported as working fine. The user then clarified that the aircraft layout is Quad-X and that the motor set consists of four 2200 KV motors plus one additional KV1000 (2810-01D) motor.

The power discussion covered the ESC's three-wire lead and BEC. The red wire on a typical ESC servo lead may provide regulated 5 V, while the thick battery leads carry the main battery voltage. The hex board may share the 5 V rail across its servo headers. Only one BEC should normally power a shared rail unless parallel operation is explicitly supported. The exact hex board battery-terminal rating still needs confirmation before applying 12 V.

The current unresolved problem is that the motors beep repeatedly and do not rotate, including during a proposed 20-second ramp from low to high throttle. The next correct step is not to increase throttle further. It is to identify the ESC model, verify the board's power topology, and test one ESC/motor at a time with a stable low-throttle signal and propellers removed.

======================================================================
PART 7 - USER QUESTIONS AND DIRECT ANSWERS (QUICK REFERENCE)
======================================================================

Q: How do I connect the ESP32 to the hex board?
A: Connect each ESP32 GPIO to the corresponding channel signal input, and connect ESP32 GND to hex-board GND. Confirm the exact board pin labels.

Q: Which channels do I need?
A: AIL, ELE, THR, RUDD, and AUX.

Q: What PWM should I use?
A: The project used 50 Hz / 20 ms frames with 1000-2000 microsecond pulses. Confirm the ESC's supported input range.

Q: What were my original pins?
A: GPIO14, 27, 26, 25, 33.

Q: What are my updated pins?
A: AIL GPIO27, ELE GPIO26, THR GPIO25, RUDD GPIO33, AUX GPIO32.

Q: Why did the hex board say NO ROLL INPUT?
A: It was not detecting a valid AIL/roll input. Check signal wiring, common ground, pulse timing, and input voltage requirements.

Q: Why did it then say NO YAW INPUT / NO SIGNAL?
A: Other required channels were not being detected. Test all required channels continuously, not just AIL.

Q: Why did THR show 44 when all channels were set to 1500?
A: 1500 microseconds is center/mid-range, not zero throttle. A typical low-throttle command is around 1000 microseconds.

Q: What is a BEC?
A: Battery Eliminator Circuit, a regulated power supply often built into an ESC that provides 5 V for receiver/control electronics.

Q: Why does the hex board show 5 V when an ESC 3-pin connector is plugged in?
A: The ESC's BEC red wire may be feeding the shared +5 V rail on the hex board. Verify the exact board routing and labels.

Q: Can I connect 12 V?
A: Only to an input explicitly rated for that battery voltage or to the ESC's thick battery leads. Never connect 12 V to a 5 V servo rail.

Q: Why do the motors beep but not rotate?
A: The ESC may not be armed, may not see valid low throttle, may need calibration, or may have a wiring/power/signal issue. The exact beep meaning depends on ESC model.

Q: Will a 20-second ramp fix the beeping?
A: No. A ramp cannot fix an ESC that has not armed or is not recognizing the signal. First establish valid low-throttle arming on one ESC.

Q: Is the Quad-X mixer ready?
A: A generic mixer was discussed, but actual motor numbering, orientation, and rotation directions must be confirmed before using it.

